Better: recognize that "do not split" is itself a merge declaration. A rule-based way: the NEG exemption should only apply when the negation token is one of the *rule* words (`never|forbid|avoid|reject|leak`) AND the phrase is quoted, OR when the token is `separate|split|distinct` and it appears *before* the phrase as an instruction ("separate C0 and C2 schemas"). "do not split" contains `not` + `split` — the word "split" is present but preceded by "not". So refine: exemption requires `sep_token` (separate|split|distinct) OR quote-context, and explicitly *not* when the sep_token is negated ("not split", "don't split", "never split"). That is: if the context matches `\b(not|never|no|don't|do not)\s+(split|separate|distinct)\b` → do NOT exempt.

Minimal implementation:
```python
NEG = re.compile(r"never|not\b|no\b|forbid|avoid|separate|split|distinct|reject|leak", re.I)
ANTI = re.compile(r"\b(?:not|never|no)\s+(?:split|separate|distinct)\b", re.I)
...
